  "content": {
    "id": "p12-problem-2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "[Optimisation problem]\n\nA burger truck normally sells 60 burgers a day for $\\$10$ per burger. The owner is advised that they should increase the price of the burger. However, for every 50 cents extra that the burger costs, the number of daily burger sales will drop by 2. The owner decides to increase the price in 50-cent increments. Let $x$ be the number of 50-cent price increases.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p12-problem-2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the daily revenue is $R(x)=(30-x)(20+x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$R(x)=(60-2x)(10+0.50x)=(30-x)(20+x)$.",
          "worked": "After $x$ increases, the price is $(10+0.50x)$ dollars per burger and daily sales are $60-2x$ burgers.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}R(x)&=(60-2x)(10+0.50x)\\\\R(x)&=2(30-x)\\cdot\\frac{20+x}{2}\\\\R(x)&=(30-x)(20+x)\\end{align*}$$\n\nRevenue is measured in dollars per day.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-problem-2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the price that the owner should charge per burger in order to maximise the daily revenue, and state what the maximum daily revenue is.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p12-problem-2-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Charge ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\$12.50",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " per burger for a maximum daily revenue of ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\$625",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p12-problem-2-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "R(x)=625-(x-5)^2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is greatest at five 50-cent increases.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}R(x)&=(30-x)(20+x)\\\\R(x)&=600+10x-x^2\\\\R(x)&=625-(x-5)^2\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $(x-5)^2\\geq0$, the maximum revenue is $\\$625$ at $x=5$. This is an allowed whole number of 50-cent increases.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Price}&=10+0.50(5)\\\\\\text{Price}&=12.50\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe owner should charge $\\$12.50$ per burger. Daily sales are $60-2(5)=50$ burgers, giving $50\\times\\$12.50=\\$625$ per day.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      }
    ]
